e going to be different this time  well i think they'll be a bit different  because  in the last 20 years  um the majority the vast majority of  people in afghanistan particularly women  but very many men as well have learned  to enjoy  their freedoms  and  they're going to be resisting  having those taken away  also  the country is far more reliant on the  west than ever it was i mean just for  example there were actually no mobile  telephones there 20 years ago  everybody's going to become a mobile  telephone now and that requires a  reliance on overseas technical  technology  they'll also be under pressure not only  from the west but also from russia and  china although russia and china  want to  make a point against the west  they are also  petrified about islamic islamic  extremism in their own countries as well  as  in neighboring afghanistan so they will  be under greater pressure  but the question is have they feel like  changed their spots i mean my instinct  is they they haven't said they'll be  that their instinct  will be to do what they did before a key  to how they behave is the attitude of  pakistan and it's not off sufficiently  discussed this  but just bear in mind that the  intelligence inter-services intelligence  directorate of pakistan  part of their defense operation  has been  up to its neck in ventriloquising  and supporting the taliban over decades  so their attitude matters as well  what would you what would you do about  pakistan ben i mean  what how can the west use its influence  in this respect uh with difficulty but  but i mean partly listen to them and  take their advice i mean  because after  the reason they have this extremely  ambiguous relationship  with the uh taliban and with other  extremists on their north west frontier  is  is not necessarily because that the very  modern people in lahore in islamabad and  karachi  support them but they're scared of them  and it's about how you as it were  tame this tiger so listen to them i 